.TH WM-ROAD 1
.SH NAME
wm/road \- moving map of where the GPS receiver is
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wm/road
[
.B -w
.I metres
] [
.I mapfile ...
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Road
draws a map around the position
.IR gpsfs (4)
gives at
.BR /mnt/gps :
a grid of latitude and longitude, a scale bar, the track so far, the
receiver's accuracy as a circle about the position and its course as an
arrow, and the lines and marks of the map files given and of every file
in
.BR /lib/road .
While it follows, each fix moves the map to keep the position at its
middle.
The status line gives the position, altitude, speed, course, accuracy
and satellites, or why there is no fix; with no receiver it says so and
tries again.
.B -w
sets the width of ground the window shows, in metres (2000).
.PP
Button 1 drags the map, which stops following.
Button 2 is the menu:
.BR follow ,
.BR "zoom in" ,
.B "zoom out"
(halving and doubling the width), and
.BR "clear track" .
The wheel zooms;
.BR + ,
.B -
and
.B f
do as the menu does.
.SS Map files
Text, a command a line;
.B #
begins a comment.
.TP
.BI line " \fR[\fPname\fR]\fP"
begins a line; each
.I "lat lon"
after it is a point of it.
.TP
.BI mark " lat lon \fR[\fPname\fR]\fP"
one place.
.PP
Degrees, north and east positive.
.SH FILES
.I Road
serves files of its own (see
.IR appfs (2)),
posted as
.BR #sroad/fs
.RB ( fs.1 ,
and so on, for another
.IR road ):
.TF marks
.TP
.B view
.BI center " lat lon" ,
.BI width " metres" ,
.B follow on
or
.BR off ,
a line each.
.TP
.B marks
one line a mark:
.I "lat lon name"
.TP
.B ctl
a read gives the view; a write is lines of the view's form, which
change it
.RB ( center
stops following), or
.BI mark " lat lon name" ,
.BI unmark " name" ,
.B clear
(the track),
.BI load " mapfile" .
A command that is not one, or a place that is no place, is refused
with the reason, and the commands after it are not done.
.TP
.B event
a line,
.BR changed ,
when the view or the marks change by a command or the mouse; following
the receiver is not a change.
.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
; mount -A '#sroad/fs' /mnt/road
; echo mark 40.6892 -74.0445 Liberty > /mnt/road/ctl
; echo width 500 > /mnt/road/ctl
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/wm/road.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR gpsfs (4),
.IR location (3)
.br
Plan 9's
.IR road (7),
in its second edition, which drew the Census Bureau's TIGER map and
followed
.IR gps (7)'s
log.
.SH BUGS
It brings no map: the lines are those of map files.
The grid and the distances assume a sphere and a flat map about the
middle, which is near enough across a town and less so across a
country.
